data.content:
All three code snippets hit EF Core 10 breaking changes. Here's what breaks and the fixes:

---

### 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — no longer accepts `Expression<Func<...>>`

The parameter changed from an expression tree to a plain `Func<...>`. Your manual `Expression.Call` / `Expression.Lambda` code **won't compile**.

**Fix** — replace the entire block with a simple lambda:

```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

No expression-tree manipulation needed anymore.

---

### 2. Complex type column names — two changes

**a) Same-named properties are now uniquified.** `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street` previously shared a column silently (data corruption risk). EF 10 appends a number (e.g. `Street`, `Street1`). This will generate a migration that **renames columns**.

**Fix** — pin explicit column names to preserve the old schema (or accept the new one):

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress,
        p => p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress,
        p => p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
});
```

**b) Nested complex types now use the full path.** `Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City` was mapped to column `BillingAddress_City`; now it becomes `Payment_BillingAddress_City`.

**Fix** — pin old names explicitly:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
```

Or accept the new names and generate a migration.

---

### 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

Every method (`CommandReaderExecuting`, `CommandReaderExecuted`, etc.) now requires an additional `string logCommandText` parameter. This carries redacted SQL for logging (inlined constants are scrubbed unless `EnableSensitiveDataLogging` is on).

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← add this
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for actual SQL
    }
}
```

Update **all** methods on the interface — `CommandScalarExecuting`, `CommandNonQueryExecuting`, and their `*Executed` counterparts all gained the same parameter.
